(Two shapes that make the call edge close on itself. A function that calls
 ITSELF with the list it was handed, and two functions that call each other
 with it, both have to settle rather than chase each other; the verdict for
 a list parameter only ever climbs - no write, one proven type, then mixed -
 so the pass runs out of moves. And a write through an ALIAS the callee took
 of its parameter is a write into the caller's list: both names are the same
 block. docs/BUGS_FOUND.md #97.)

To 'countdown into' with a list called noted and a number called n.
  If n is greater than 0 then,
    append n to noted,
    'countdown into' of noted and n subtract 1.

To 'ping into' with a list called noted and a number called n.
  If n is greater than 0 then,
    append "ping" to noted,
    'pong into' of noted and n subtract 1.

To 'pong into' with a list called noted and a number called n.
  If n is greater than 0 then,
    append "pong" to noted,
    'ping into' of noted and n subtract 1.

To 'stash through an alias' with a list called noted and a text called label.
  a list called kept is the noted.
  append label to kept.

(Straight recursion: every write is a number, and the caller had proven
 nothing, so the reads go by tag and still come back as numbers.)
a list called counted is [].
'countdown into' of counted and 3.
Print "counted: {counted}".
Print "counted last: {counted's last}".

(Mutual recursion: each function inherits what the other writes.)
a list called sounded is [].
'ping into' of sounded and 4.
Print "sounded: {sounded}".
Print "sounded last: {sounded's last}".

(A write through an alias of the parameter.)
a list called noted is [].
'stash through an alias' of noted and "aliased".
Print "noted last: {noted's last}".
